#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172979} :activity/id #uuid "dd0bd3a2-aeb8-58a5-aac7-31019071b253" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048885 :topic/id #uuid "c96ee93a-fd82-595c-824f-e11934066ffc" :topic/math-academy-id 3350} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172016 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171820 :question/id #uuid "8a1be39d-6621-5d71-b1a4-3669d210ee8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6986"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3e3fdc8-67d0-5b46-9c6b-c1ec8d4ea24f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536850}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Asymptotes"} :step/id #uuid "c590d4ee-83a9-5629-b80d-5b9bf0fef93a" :step/math-academy-id 25710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172869 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172017 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171821 :question/id #uuid "f43aebf0-6aa9-58cf-9475-69f5af74d71f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6988"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1616167b-5679-5ff6-b385-3e1b59352c64" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048272}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536881}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "df729ab0-2123-5f4d-8bcf-9b5d2fb448d1" :step/math-academy-id 25712 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172868}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172018 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171822 :question/id #uuid "4dabeee5-1c46-5198-9c51-9099fb5df617" :question/math-academy-id "e-7126"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "244419de-9b13-5923-b0a5-1bdc6f9b03b2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536893}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"} :step/id #uuid "cf643e99-f61d-5e66-8afd-c5e23c2c54df" :step/math-academy-id 26196 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172869}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172979 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173195 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the differential equation $y' = -4xy.$ The slope field for this equation is shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a47ec9497553fdcc9d20607f309bdaee00bb1fc0af4d39dea8a6a843a2e417e6.png)\n\nLet's see how much information we can gather about the differential equation by referring *only* to its slope field.\n\n- First, note that the slope field has neither vertical nor horizontal symmetry. Therefore, the associated differential equation must be of the form $y' = f(x,y).$ Indeed, the equation $y' =-4xy$ is of this form.\n\n- Second, notice that the solution curves appear to have stationary points along the lines $x=0$ and $y=0.$ This means that the right-hand side of our differential equation must evaluate to zero on these lines. Indeed, this is true of our equation.\n\n![](images/7b/7beccfd6840c2715bfd3c923f4fe6ac05bdf07f9ed65931b016efd937b1a0b63.png)\n\n- Third, the lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ split the plane into four regions. The slopes of the solution curves in each of these regions are summarized in the table below.\n\n| $y > 0$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <       0$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ |\n|  | $x<       0$ | $x> 0$ |\n\nNotice that the sign of $f(x,y)$ in the four regions agrees with the above table:\n\n- $f(x,y)=-4xy$ is positive in the second and fourth quadrants, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=-4xy$ is negative in the first and third quadrants." :tutorial/id #uuid "dd16b3e1-53b3-55bf-af84-540daaefde15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5672 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9018b840-14fc-5403-a85b-0696a121416b" :step/math-academy-id 34802 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172982}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172082 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171888 :question/id #uuid "95746e3f-5cdf-58e2-8dbb-39b3cbe5cfc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9356"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd82d96b-34d5-5d20-b810-7ea88b0b5797" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050480}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332803}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Stationary Points"} :step/id #uuid "de33aa28-2c66-5a35-b972-8287ff822b59" :step/math-academy-id 35034 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172870}}] :activity/title "Analyzing Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172172}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536925} :activity/id #uuid "430421d0-b0f8-5943-9c2b-aea2664e5f42" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048885 :topic/id #uuid "c96ee93a-fd82-595c-824f-e11934066ffc" :topic/math-academy-id 3350} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4302042c-8055-53af-bd3a-be8fd087c821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536385} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6c4c42-b7c6-5745-94ba-7c3cfe7a7411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536416} {:answer/id #uuid "71203ad7-761f-586b-91f4-ea2287a2cb0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x+x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536472} {:answer/id #uuid "8a594017-dd00-5e85-8323-b197a7f40e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536508} {:answer/id #uuid "d38d8b71-d94d-5a16-84ce-3da13f0fbb57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x-1)^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536616}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536416} :answer-field/id #uuid "41f0f8e8-48f1-5e24-ab08-3b3bcbba4475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a2ee811e-ffbe-53ee-9b06-888771cc6f39" :question/math-academy-id "q-86442" :question/problem "![](images/90/9000c061a8826c4492400671b3ee8e6700420e1e553ceda47e6f086a474211c2.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a7/a71217d2b2e610b38f78f4471cc122de7098708900e3658cda449118df0e63dd.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(y+1)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>-1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n \nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(y+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "082ae2ae-0aa5-5392-9f40-e5a1a5b40f7e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536922 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536893 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0026c51e-4d80-5bc2-8461-49af596e4e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x-1)(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536285} {:answer/id #uuid "3c7b084c-10ab-51da-a2b3-418e19f04fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536378} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b99d07-755c-5bef-bc57-98238ed75afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x+1)(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536591} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf33ee7-68ac-5522-9117-9e92aa0afd2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x+1)^{2}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536654} {:answer/id #uuid "ffca65b3-4207-5784-b911-da4d96590ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x+1)(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536694}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536591} :answer-field/id #uuid "476162ce-d7d2-51ce-9444-b2f25f2ae279" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e7d6da00-83dc-5f7a-b7e3-c9a339c8d8c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-50157" :question/problem "![](images/b4/b45ab87c3b5f6b083ca5828676ca96486e3825b3b3fbbfc3f31d19fa65a215b5.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ef/ef837c5bdd4198651302e77b70d592c7771071a1ee3543ce76a3685b0439fa82.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-1$ and $y=-2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(x+1)(y+2) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(x+1)^{2}(y+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-1$ and $y=-2$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>-2$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-2$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x+1)(y+2).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4063bcf2-9c80-51dd-b5c4-c46157272fde" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536970}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536925 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007e39a0-05eb-578a-a48d-5694fcd3c657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536286} {:answer/id #uuid "4a90b7e9-8188-53a6-95fc-db777d59155f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536400} {:answer/id #uuid "6324d0c2-5334-554d-8b13-506fc5b5dbb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536452} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9da200-0ade-54bf-b021-af17d6df52d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536541} {:answer/id #uuid "aaff3195-01f3-5afb-88f1-a42822d0cbe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536559} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb57608e-2ee3-5d75-9465-c30eb8072dad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8972fb0a-9f6e-50fc-86fb-a54e4588df90" :question/math-academy-id "q-50133" :question/problem "![](images/93/9389d3d0d9206b72f379a328819e4624da3a294fc3222f048edc53957a8679ac.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." :question/worked-solution "![](images/31/310d05d05284201c4914de171e6ee6a7574784da4c9bf7d4e442cdf2a210722d.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope lines on the same vertical line are all the same. This means that the slope does not vary with $y.$ In other words, ${y}^{′}$ is a function of $x$ only.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & {y}^{′}={e}^{x} \\\\  & {y}^{′}={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become vertical as $x\\to 0.$ \n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{1}{x}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$ become vertical as $x\\to 0.$\nBy contrast, \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "44f60c0c-5d2f-59e4-aaaa-1e285c4c8203" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536922}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172601 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "875cf4b7-338f-5817-becc-33f4febb98a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171232} {:answer/id #uuid "b83a571d-3549-53c7-9c4c-b743d0a7d28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171233} {:answer/id #uuid "0c7be0cf-b60a-5cec-89be-3ab4b2abe9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171234} {:answer/id #uuid "180ca74f-c815-53df-94f3-03f284c6cfa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171235} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea92053-c8d8-51bb-b37e-a5e88dc24a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171236} :answer-field/id #uuid "e04bb66b-30ad-5845-a229-f15ee09b5f34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d0a5efdc-b40e-5dde-9c9f-7f214338581c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80708" :question/problem "![](images/32/32165a622d57d6af278b7ce288093ef4db6867619046b8625071f0274a1c7e93.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}y+{x}^{3}$\n\nII. ${y}^{′}=xy-x$\n\nIII. ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a2/a278676af009b7096bd50f9b3918f0e8090c1822a9e029ac2b4b6325d1ba7a4c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>-1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}y+{x}^{3}={x}^{3}(y+1)\n$$\n\n evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-x=x(y-1)\n$$\n\n evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1.$\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(y+1)\n$$\n\n evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "cffe8872-3258-5dea-971d-7ad093112cb1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536976}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12ba6730-d2d1-5ea5-80ef-8e8c38878511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536314} {:answer/id #uuid "2542a06d-9303-52fb-97e7-add9f98d7b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536339} {:answer/id #uuid "2eee5914-1ac4-5e4e-bd6b-cdcfec586af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536354} {:answer/id #uuid "9a697427-3416-5d0c-94de-b62ae70838f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=2x+2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536532} {:answer/id #uuid "bc223e25-78a4-519a-96e6-70f92377c549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536582}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536339} :answer-field/id #uuid "a19fb674-3a6d-5382-99ac-6066604aff59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cd64c565-f297-5346-b626-26857f890c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-80709" :question/problem "![](images/84/84597fa3c8e0a187df5792e8a8289033e6549599b1df5a34073049a5b3322770.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/36/3673d384035d7febd8540e34143c2a490f7aa3968752785ef809cff16d2524ce.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-2x=x(x-2) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}={x}^{2}(2-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x<2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n\n \nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x=x(x-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}-2x.$"} :step/id #uuid "df0c9231-2d00-5a28-90a3-a4c777823c60" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536905}}] :activity/title "Analyzing Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536271}]}
